   Test the failure and retry paths without running
   destructive or unrelated real tasks.

   Test the generated Git commit messages.

   Make sure the imperative prompt body is wrapped at
   72 columns without destroying its meaning.

   Verify that world files accurately reflect installations.

   Test uninstall in an isolated HOME or disposable prefix
   before allowing it to remove a real ~/world.

   Don't run a destructive uninstall against the user's
   existing ~/world merely to demonstrate that it works.

   Ensure that temporary build outputs and machine-specific
   runtime files are appropriately ignored by Git and
   Obsidian Sync where applicable.

   Don't ignore source files, prompts, or documentation
   that are supposed to be shared.

   Keep the repository usable by multiple humans on
   different machines.

   Make sure nothing requires hardcoded paths to one
   particular person's home directory or checkout.

   Don't make unrelated architectural changes merely
   because we're refactoring the directory structure.

   When implementation details are ambiguous, prefer
   established Unix, Git, Gentoo, Node.js, and Electron
   conventions over custom machinery.

   When a standard solution already exists, use it.

   If something genuinely cannot be completed, leave
   the system in a working state, document the blocker,
   and add the unfinished work to COVENANT.

   Don't silently substitute a stub for functionality
   that can reasonably be implemented.

   Finally, give a concise report of what changed,
   what was tested, what works, and what remains.

   The goal is a system in which humans write brief
   English descriptions of what they want, agents turn
   those descriptions into executable English drafts,
   agents ask questions where necessary, humans answer
   them and review the final prompts, the prompts run,
   humans inspect and accept the results, and the system
   documents itself in both imperative and declarative
   form before committing the changes.

   The code is the implementation.

   The imperative history says what we asked to happen.

   The declarative documentation says what is now true.

   And Git connects all three.

